y within  a state government  environmental agency and so most of the  work that I was involved with was  actually at the state or regional or  landscape scale and it was was all about  planning and decision-making so just to  give you a bit of that that background  on where I've come from I've actually  come from a background where I've been  involved in in real world  decision-making at the landscape scale  Loretha at the regional scale and so my  interests in thinking at whole continent  scale or global scales is relatively  recent okay so this can happen at  different spatial scales we can also  think of state of biodiversity as being  assessed in relation to different  dimensions and levels of biodiversity  and so there I've just got an adaptation  of the old Reno's perspective on on on  the dimensions and levels of  biodiversity and when when we look at  that and we think back to to Woody's  talk then it becomes obvious that there  are there are some levels and dimensions  of biodiversity that a lot easier to  assess remotely than others so things  like ecosystem structure and function  people are doing a reasonably good job  of assessing those remotely but when we  come to composition other than some of  those things that would he mentioned  like um the aristocratic be trees for  example that you can now assess to some  extent with hyperspectral imagery most  of the composition of biodiversity of  the species and genetic level quite  obviously we're not in a position to  assess that remotely so this presents  some some very real challenges and so  I'm going to be focusing most of my talk  on this compositional dimension of  biodiversity and on the challenge  of assessing that at the global scale  and if we look at how people have have  approached this this problem before  there are two commonly employed  approaches in global assessments of  compositional biodiversity one of which  is to look at a tour to work with  distributions for example range maps of  species in better known biological  groups such as the the vertebrates and  Walter talked a lot about this yesterday  and another fairly commonly used  approaches one where people focus on  mapped ecosystem types of biomes or or  ecoregions and essentially use those as  course filter surrogates for  biodiversity composition and use  approaches like species area  relationships to infer what's happening  in terms of the retention of  compositional diversity but there's been  a lot of work done over the years and I  was involved a lot with this sort of  about twenty years ago of evaluating how  well some of these surrogate approaches  perform for the unsurveyed elements of  biodiversity or now or in other words  the other 99 percent of biodiversity so  all of the you know the invertebrates  the lower and higher plants that the  microbes and so on what how well are  these catered for by these these  surrogate approaches and I think you  know to to summarize that the story  there the the results of a lot of those  assessments have been have been quite  mixed so there are some real issues with  using things like vertebrate species  ranges and broadly mapped ecosystem  types and ecoregions and so on as  surrogates for the rest of biodiversity  or the other 99 percent but then on the  other hand we know that for a lot of  these neglected groups there's a there's  a surprising amount of information out  there and this is just one example of a  group that we were just starting to do  some modeling with at the moment  globally the the diptera or true flies  and when you look at that group there  are over three million records for  almost 120,000 species of of diptera  within GBF there's also now quite a  superb high-level phylogeny of the whole  dip Duras so this is quite an amazing  amount of information on a group like  that okay but as you would have seen  from that that map on the left there the  geographic coverage of those records is  is patchy in there that there are big  gaps so we don't have geographically  complete data hence the idea of  integrating in situ or field surveyed  biological data with remotely mapped  environmental variables and Woody's  already talked about this quite a bit  and and this can be used the most basic  level just a model spatial pattern in  the distribution of biodiversity if we  get a little bit more sophisticated we  can bring in remotely observe change in  ecosystem state or intactness to infer  changes in the status of biodiversity  and then in turn we can get down here to  bringing in projected pressures and  responses things like climate change  land-use change to project biodiversity  into the future so Woody's talked a fair  bit about this so i won't i won't dwell  on that too long but just jump straight  to this slide which is just simply to to  point out that you know as soon as you  mentioned linking biological data and  remotely mapped environmental data a lot  of people immediately assume that you're  talking about species distribution  modeling or or niche modeling that's  that's certainly one very important  strategy for linking these sources of  information and I've spent a lot of my  career deriving species distribution  models I modeled my first species in  1982 and  model many thousands of species in that  in the 15 or 20 years following that but  speech distribution modeling is just one  way of integrating these sources of  information and a lot of options there  that I won't go into if you're  interested in learning more about that  then have a look at that review paper  that I wrote with Antoine he signed a  few years ago and journal of applied  ecology but I just wanted to focus on  the two ends of the spectrum there one  end is the individual species  distribution modeling that we're all  very familiar with but down at the other  end of the spectrum here is is another  approach which we're now using quite  heavily is it which is what we call  macro macro ecological modelling of  collective biodiversity properties and  so this is modeling things like richness  and and compositional turnover across  space and i'll be talking about this in  a little bit more detail soon but the  point I wanted to make here is that you  know we've got all of these different  modeling strategies and its really  horses for the courses that the  appropriateness of different strategies  depends very much on the question of  interest and the nature of the  biological group that you're working  with and how much data you've got within  that group so if you're if you're  working with a group like birds where  society places value on each and every  bird species and they all have names  that people are familiar with and so on  and you've got a reasonable number of  records per species then it makes sense  to be modeling individual species  distributions but if you get down to  this end of the spectrum here where you  know the group you're working on people  aren't really valuing the individual  species within it but we do value the  overall diversity of that group and also  we've got a huge number of species in  that group a lot of them are very few  records and probably a lot that don't  have any records at all and we don't  even know that those species exist then  this other kind of strategy might make a  lot more  sense in that situation so what do I  mean by modeling collective properties  biodiversity this is probably fairly  obvious that the one that has been done  a lot more is where people model local  richness or alpha diversity so the  something like the species diversity at  a location as a function of the  environment of let that location the  biogeographic history human disturbance  and and so on or it becomes a lot more  interesting when we start to also bring  in consideration of the compositional  dissimilarity between locations or in  other words beater or beta diversity and  trying to model that as a function of  differences in the abiotic environment  between those locations the level of bio  geographic isolation and those those  sorts of things now with with all of  this modeling we can be talking about  just simple measures of species richness  or compositional dissimilarity in terms  of species overlap or we can get a  little bit more sophisticated and  bringing considerations of phylogeny so  all of these modeling approaches have  have phylogenetic versions of them now  just to bring this to life a bit here's  an example of work that we've done  within the whole Australian continent  going back a couple of years now we're  here we're focusing primarily on  modeling those those patterns of beta  diversity or patterns of compositional  turnover across the Australian continent  and this is a rather different approach  to the individual species modeling  approach in that this is a model in this  case here and sorry if you can't see  those those fitted functions there  they've obviously very faint on the  screen but the important point is that  this this is a model that's working with  compositional turnover as a collective  property of this particular  group which for the example I've got  here is is land snails and we've modeled  lots and lots of different biological  groups within Australia but you know  this is a model that's fitted to a whole  lot of data for almost 3,000 species of  land snails so we're looking at all the  data for land snails together within a  single model relating that to  environmental variables to do with  climate terrains soils also bringing  consideration of geographic isolation  into the modeling and this is all used  to then say something about the spatial  pattern in the distribution of  biodiversity for that particular group  in this case the land stones across the  Australian continent and so that then in  turn is used if we do that for a lot of  different biological groups that then  provides the basis for doing all sort of  sorts of assessments that are built on  top of that t