 that the flavor of what might be  possible hope hoping to then leverage  the sorts of resources that are needed  to really roll this out properly so in  this case and I won't have time to go  into it in great detail we're bringing  together the best available data on all  the fern species for the world over 1.3  million records for about 10,000 species  of ferns from from gb combining that  with best available environmental data  land-use change layers at a fairly  coarse resolution from the the image  model climate change projections  bringing all this together with modeling  approaches that I won't go into in  detail now but happy to talk about them  with anyone later bringing all that  together to then say something about  what we think is happening to the  attention of compositional diversity  within this group okay that that then  allows us to to say something about the  expected changes at different spatial  resolutions I won't try to explain that  one in detail but the important point is  that because of the way we do this and  the way we're considering our for beta  and gamma diversity we can in fact  aggregate up these results to different  spatial scales so we can say something  about what we think is happening for  example with an individual ecoregion  with a whole biogeographic realm or even  with with the whole planet ok just to  very quickly run through what are some  of the current directions in the  application of these these kinds of  approaches and some of this has already  been touched on by others so i won't i  won't dwell on parts of it but in terms  of that the types of biological data  that we can use in this kind of of  modeling clearly you know citizen  science is changing the whole ballgame  there but the other big change now is is  environmental metagenomics and we're  working a lot now with people who are  doing metagenomics not just for microbes  but also now for things like arthropods  and you know this is a way of going out  and getting these big grab samples of  the environment using fast throughput  sequencing to get a complete profile of  the biodiversity within those those  samples so this is this is a big step  forward woody's done a superb job of  covering the remote sensing end of  things I won't dwell on this but  obviously hyperspectral lidar and radar  again changes and there's a lot of very  exciting work happening with the  derivation of new fine scaled a biotic  surfaces climate and soils etc I've just  got one example they're drawn from work  of some of my CSIRO colleagues  in Canberra who are all linked into this  big new global initiative in fact  CSIRO's one of the leaders of the global  soil map initiative which is trying to  transform the way that soil attributes  are mapped globally and so they have  been trialing approaches that are going  to be rolled out globally for mapping  soil attributes from remote sensing  integrated with a whole lot of other  sources of information and various forms  of modeling so that's that's a very  exciting step forwards in terms of  directions in modeling spatial pattern  in the distribution of biodiversity  we've been doing a lot of work on  smarter ways of incorporating phylogeny  accounting for by geographic history and  also dealing with the ever-present  issues of a bias than incompleteness in  biological sampling we've also been  doing a lot of work on on getting  smarter about how we do biodiversity  projections under global change  scenarios incorporating ecological  mechanisms and processes into our models  and also starting to get serious about  accounting for the capacity for  evolutionary adaptation in these  projections and if I could just flash up  a couple of examples of that this is  work led by one of my my postdocs from  the past three years where he this is  Carell McCarney he's been extending the  sorts of macro ecological models that  we've been used to project future  changes in biodiversity he's been  extending those models to better  consider aspects of ecological processes  so so so that's it so in this case  community assembly rules and and and  dispersal are actually incorporated into  that modeling and in terms of i  mentioned capacity for evolutionary  adaptation this is work that is just  starting up and this is a very big  project a joint project between CSIRO  Melbourne University and monash  university which is looking at the  genomics of climate adaptation and  that's actually using a number of of  model groups of organisms to a  Drosophila is one eucalyptus is another  and there are a few more but but  essentially they're working on getting a  lot smarter about being able to to  predict estimate or infer the evolution  evil the capacity for evolutionary  adaptation across some different sets of  organisms and so the the drosophila are  quite an extraordinary group to work  with in this regard I think woody was  talking about the whole enchilada before  the drosophila are definitely the whole  enchilada they have for within Australia  for the drosophila group they they know  absolutely everything you would ever  want to know about this group in terms  of what it does in or what climate  change does to the group right through  to running experimental evolution under  climate change so it's it's quite  amazing and this is going to provide the  most superb testbed for then evaluating  all sorts of other approaches the people  have used to model biological responses  to the climate change okay then finally  this box out to the side here which i  haven't mentioned so far which is a  critically important one and that is the  the evaluation and calibration of some  of these sort of remotely derived models  of what's happening to biodiversity and  evaluating and calibrating knows using  long term field monitoring plots  ecological plots and biodiversity plots  a really crucial part of the whole story  and then there's also potential here to  to make use of things like paleo  eco